+*The class has clinical trial experience in sarcoma, and this menu did not previously record it.*
+Two published trials give proteasome inhibition to sarcoma patients outside EMC. Both are read here
+at abstract level only: no full text was retrieved for either, so enrolment denominators beyond
+those the abstracts state, per-histology outcomes and response rates are unread rather than absent.
+Maki and colleagues ran a multicentre phase II of bortezomib monotherapy in recurrent or metastatic
+sarcoma [21]. Its abstract reports one confirmed partial response among 21 evaluable patients on the
+soft-tissue-sarcoma arm, in a patient with leiomyosarcoma; states that the study closed after the
+first stage of accrual for inactivity; and concludes that bortezomib has minimal single-agent
+activity in soft-tissue sarcoma and that any further sarcoma study should pair it with agents
+showing demonstrated preclinical synergy. Whether any EMC patient was enrolled is unread. Boklan and
+colleagues ran a phase I of carfilzomib with cyclophosphamide and etoposide in relapsed or
+refractory leukaemia and non-CNS solid tumours, in patients aged 6 months to under 30 years [22]:
+38 patients were treated, 24 of them in the solid-tumour stratum; no maximum tolerated dose was
+reached in that stratum; a recommended phase 2 dose was defined; and the authors state that patients
+with sarcomas benefited most, warranting further evaluation. That is a dose-finding trial which is
+not sarcoma-specific, and whose abstract carries no response rate, no comparator and no sarcoma
+subgroup denominator. It is not an efficacy result, and neither trial is EMC data.
+
+Neither trial settles this candidate in either direction. The 2005 study tested bortezomib, a
+different agent from carfilzomib in a different era and regimen, as a single agent, whereas the
+ex-vivo signal carrying this candidate is a combination signal; a negative monotherapy result for
+one member of a class is not evidence that carfilzomib fails in this disease. Read the other way,
+that trial's own closing recommendation — combination with agents showing demonstrated preclinical
+synergy — is the shape of the ex-vivo result reported by others [13], and this review does not treat
+that correspondence as support for activity. What the two records do settle is the novelty claim:
+proteasome inhibition is untried in EMC but is not clinically untried in sarcoma, and the rows in
+section 3 are written accordingly.
+
